id: codex
name: Codex

Configure llm.birks.dev as a custom model provider for Codex, over the OpenAI Responses API.

--- file ~/.codex/config.toml (toml) ---
model = "Qwen3-Coder-30B"
model_provider = "llm-birks-dev"

[model_providers.llm-birks-dev]
name = "llm.birks.dev"
base_url = "https://llm.birks.dev/v1"
env_key = "LLM_BIRKS_DEV_API_KEY"
wire_api = "responses"

--- commands (sh) ---
export LLM_BIRKS_DEV_API_KEY="YOUR_API_KEY"

codex

--- commands (PowerShell) ---
$env:LLM_BIRKS_DEV_API_KEY = "YOUR_API_KEY"

codex

--- notes ---
- env_key names the environment variable Codex reads the credential from.
- Recent Codex (0.122+) only supports wire_api = "responses"; the older "chat" value fails at startup. This provider therefore targets the endpoint's OpenAI Responses API at /v1/responses.
- If Codex errors on startup with a 404 or an unsupported-endpoint message, this model's server build does not expose /v1/responses. Point base_url at a small Responses-to-Chat proxy (e.g. LiteLLM) instead; the other clients here use Chat Completions directly and need no proxy.
- If you already have a config.toml, merge these keys instead of overwriting it.

--- agent prompt ---
Configure Codex on this machine to use my self-hosted llm.birks.dev endpoint at https://llm.birks.dev. Add a [model_providers.*] section to ~/.codex/config.toml with wire_api = "responses" and env_key pointing at my environment variable, preserving my existing settings. Use the model "Qwen3-Coder-30B". Read the API key from the LLM_BIRKS_DEV_API_KEY environment variable. Do not write the key itself into any file in a git repository, and do not print it. Preserve my existing unrelated configuration.
